# This program asks rt_heap_stats() how many allocations one await made, and
# the recorded 37 is a fact about the VM's object model, not about the language.
#
# It was 26 until the suspension frame gained its lifecycle word. Ten of the
# eleven come from the word being WRITTEN, not from carrying it: the frame's
# extra field costs exactly one allocation (26 -> 27 with the field and no
# writes), and each store of a constant into a struct field costs the VM another
# — the same cost the resume-point store beside it has always had. On the native
# lane a store into a frame field is a store; this number measures the
# interpreter's boxing, which is why it is recorded for one backend only.
# The native runtime allocates differently by construction - docs/RUNTIME.md says
# so in as many words: "the native counters describe native allocation traffic
# only". It produces 13 at one worker and 16 at thirty-two, which is the same
# point made twice: the number is not portable across backends and is not even
# portable across worker counts within one backend.
#
# Recorded against the VM only, therefore, rather than reblessed with a second
# number that would freeze today's native allocation traffic as a promise.
# Native allocation traffic is watched where it means something: the carrier
# budgets and the control-lane counters, which measure it per operation against
# a threshold rather than as one absolute count.
vm
